Subject: [RFC net-next v1 0/4] net: flow_dissector: tunnel inner-flow descent + adaptive auto-enable
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

This RFC applies on top of "[PATCH net-next] net: flow_dissector: opt-in
byte-identical fast paths for common shapes" and is posted separately
because, unlike that series, patches 1-3 here change dissection output by
design. It is design discussion, not a merge request.

The operator problem
--------------------
For VXLAN / Geneve / GTP-U traffic the dissector stops at the outer
tunnel header, so every hash-based consumer (RSS, RPS, ECMP, bonding,
tc-flower, aRFS) sees the *outer* tunnel identity, not the inner flow.
We measured the effect directly on the real flow_hash_from_keys()
(userspace port of the mainline dissector), feeding a single
tunnel-endpoint pair carrying 2000 distinct inner flows, 20000 packets.
Numbers are the count of distinct hashes (flow-identity resolution) and
the 256-bucket occupancy (an RSS indirection table):

  GTP-U (dport 2152)           outer:    1 flow,   1/256 buckets, entropy 0.00
                               inner: 2000 flows, 256/256 buckets, entropy 0.99
  VXLAN/Geneve, fixed sport    outer:    1 flow,   1/256 buckets, entropy 0.00
                               inner: 2000 flows, 256/256 buckets, entropy 0.99

All 2000 inner flows collapse onto a single hash bucket — up to 512x
hot-bucket imbalance, one queue/path doing all the work. GTP-U is the
sharpest case: it has no outer source-port entropy at all, and it is the
mobile-core data path where millions of subscriber flows ride one tunnel
between two endpoints.

Being honest about VXLAN/Geneve: modern Linux derives the outer UDP
source port from the inner flow hash, so with a Linux sender the outer
tuple already carries per-inner-flow entropy:

  VXLAN/Geneve, kernel sport   outer: 1883 flows, 256/256 buckets, entropy 0.98
                               inner: 2000 flows, 256/256 buckets, entropy 0.99

For pure hash *spreading* the outer tuple is already fine there — we are
not claiming a spreading win for kernel-sourced VXLAN. Descent's value in
that case is *identity*: a consumer that must read the actual inner flow
(tc-flower matching an inner 5-tuple for policy/offload, per-tenant
accounting, ECMP path stability) cannot use an opaque source port.

Patches 1-3: opt-in inner descent
---------------------------------
net.flow_dissector.{vxlan,geneve,gtpu}_inner, off by default. When
enabled, the fast path sets FLOW_DIS_ENCAPSULATION and dispatches the
inner Eth/IP/L4 (VXLAN/Geneve TEB) or inner IP (GTP-U) through the same
fast-path helpers, so every consumer gets the inner identity at once.
CPU is ~net-zero (the inner work cancels the outer saving); the win is
the flow distribution/identity above, and holds for IPv4 and IPv6 inner
flows and underlays.

Because the slow path stops at the outer header today, descent makes
fast-path output differ from slow-path output — a deliberate behaviour
change, never auto-enabled, excluded from the byte-identical KUnit
contract in the base series.

Like the base series' tunnel fast paths, the descents thread the
MAX_FLOW_DISSECT_HDRS budget (num_hdrs), so a crafted overlay-in-overlay
chain defers to the slow path rather than recursing without bound.

Open questions for the list:

  - Should the slow path grow the same descent, so the contract stays
    "fast == slow" and the behaviour is available without the fast path?
  - Per-netns config vs the global static_branch used here.
  - Tunnel port discovery: VXLAN is hardcoded to 4789; Cilium uses 8472,
    so production needs to consult the tunnel netdev. GTP-U handles only
    v1 G-PDU with no optional header.

Patch 4: adaptive auto-enable
-----------------------------
The base series' counters make per-shape eligible fractions observable
and the measured cost model (per-match saving S, hot-path miss cost C)
makes the enable threshold a derived number: enabling a shape is
net-positive exactly when its matching fraction exceeds
p_be = C / (S + C). Patch 4 turns that into an in-kernel "auto" mode so
the operator sets one knob instead of many. Decisions are driven by a
packet-count window (net.flow_dissector.auto_window_packets), not a
wall-clock tick: every N dissects the kernel compares each
byte-identical shape's windowed eligible fraction to its break-even and
flips the gate, with hysteresis, a multi-window dwell, and a flip rate
cap. Traffic-proportional (a busy link adapts in milliseconds, an idle
link does not churn); the hot path only samples, static_branch flips run
in a workqueue. MPLS is not auto-managed (its saving barely exceeds the
miss cost on in-order cores) and the descent gates are never
auto-managed (they change hashing — that must stay a deliberate operator
action).

The same policy can live in a userspace agent (read the stats file,
write the sysctls, identical thresholds), so this RFC asks where the
loop belongs: in-kernel single knob, or "mechanism not policy" with a
reference agent. The counters in the base series are the common
foundation for either.

Supporting data (flow-distribution study, per-shape/per-arch matrix,
break-even derivation): https://github.com/randomizedcoder/xdp2 , under
perf-results/ .

